import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'.')
from mk import mk
mk("JC-UA2C-sZU","Phase Distortion / Phase Modulation Synth in VCV Rack",
 "four-phasor-wavetable-voices-per-voice-phase-shaper-cross-pm-matrix-modulator-only-operator-morph-filter-notch",
 "Four-voice phasor-driven synth: each voice is a wavetable read by its own phasor (pitch = phasor rate) with a phase-shaper for phase distortion (curve, S-curve, split, triangle, fold), all four outputs fed back through a 4x4 matrix into each other's phase inputs for cross phase modulation, panned and mixed, then a lowpass→bandpass→highpass→notch morph filter and drive",
 "Build a playable hybrid voice that combines three ways of shaping tone: wavetable morphing, phase distortion and cross phase modulation. Drive each wavetable oscillator with a rising unipolar saw phasor (the phasor's rate sets the pitch; with no phasor there is no sound). Put a phase shaper between phasor and table: bending the phasor (curve, S-curve, split, triangle or fold modes) changes how the table is read, so a sine becomes brighter shapes without a filter. Make four such voices, mix them in stereo with per-voice pan (and pan modulation), and route all four post-shaping outputs back through a 4x4 matrix mixer into each voice's phase-modulation input so any voice can modulate any other, or itself (feedback). Use octave relationships between carrier and modulator (modulator an octave or two below, slightly detuned) and keep one voice muted as a modulator only. Finish with a filter that morphs lowpass → bandpass → highpass → notch, LFOs on cutoff and morph at different rates, drive, and reverb last. Delta beyond our single-oscillator phase-distortion and warp-mode skills: the four-voice cross-PM matrix with per-voice PD and a modulator-only voice.",
 ["Modular or grid environment with phasor, phase-driven wavetable oscillator, phase shaper, matrix mixer, multimode filter (the video uses free VCV Rack modules), or Web Audio: an AudioWorklet that keeps four phase accumulators, applies a shaping curve to each phase, reads a wavetable, and adds a scaled matrix of the other voices' previous outputs to each phase (implementation note)","LFOs with attenuverters","Reverb for the final space"],
 [("voice_count","integer","Number of phasor voices (4 in the video)",True),("pd_mode","enum","curve | S-curve | split | triangle | fold, per voice",True),("pm_matrix","table","4x4 amounts: source voice output → destination voice phase input (diagonal = feedback)",True),("octave_offsets","list","Per-voice octave relationship, e.g. carrier C4, modulator C3, deep modulator C2",False)],
 [("pd_pm_voice","patch","Four-voice PD + cross-PM instrument"),("mod_map","table","LFO assignments for PD amount, pan, cutoff and filter morph")],
 ["Phase modulation needs sample-accurate phase control. In Web Audio, build it in an AudioWorklet; OscillatorNode frequency modulation is FM, not phase modulation.","Self-feedback (a voice modulating itself) quickly turns noisy. Raise it slowly.","The video gives relationships and octave choices, not knob values. Set amounts by ear.","Module names from the video are not required. Any phasor, phase shaper, matrix mixer and multimode filter will do."],
 [("Drive a wavetable oscillator with a rising unipolar saw phasor. Confirm on a scope that there is no output without the phasor, and that the phasor's rate sets the pitch. Start on a sine frame and morph between a few basic frames.","phasor + wavetable","A pitched sine that follows the phasor rate; morphing changes the basic shape"),
  ("Insert a phase shaper between phasor and table. Raise the shape amount in curve mode, then try S-curve, split, triangle and fold modes, watching the bent phasor on the scope.","phase shaper","The same sine table sounds brighter and changes character per mode (phase distortion)"),
  ("Duplicate the voice to four voices. Mix them in a stereo mixer with per-voice pan. For unipolar pan modulation, set pan fully left and the attenuverter fully right so the modulation sweeps across the field.","4 voices + pan mixer","Four independent voices you can place and move in stereo"),
  ("Feed the four post-shaping voice outputs into a 4x4 matrix mixer and send its four outputs to the voices' phase-modulation inputs (inputs fully open). Example: listen to voice 1 only, and use voice 2 an octave down (slightly detuned) to modulate voice 1's phase; then put voice 3 two octaves down to modulate voice 2.","cross-PM matrix","Increasingly complex, metallic or growling timbres as matrix amounts rise"),
  ("Add phase distortion and a different shape on a modulator voice (for example triangle mode on voice 2, folding on voice 4) and modulate its shape and PD amount with LFOs. Keep voice 4 muted in the mix so it works only as a modulator.","modulator shaping","Modulators change the colour of the audible voices without being heard themselves"),
  ("Modulate PD amount with a slow LFO through an attenuverter; optionally modulate the matrix amounts too (for example voice 1 → voice 2) so cross-PM depth moves over time.","movement","The tone evolves while a chord is held"),
  ("Route the mix through a multimode filter that morphs lowpass → bandpass → highpass → notch. Close the cutoff, then modulate cutoff and morph with two LFOs at different rates. Add drive, choose a plucky or long amp envelope, and add reverb last.","filter + drive + reverb","A finished pad or pluck with slow spectral motion and a sense of space")],
 ["With the phase shaper at zero and the matrix at zero, each voice outputs the clean selected wavetable frame.","Raising one matrix amount (source voice → destination phase) audibly changes only the destination voice's timbre.","Muting the modulator-only voice in the mixer does not remove its effect on the voices it modulates.","Filter morph sweeps audibly through lowpass, bandpass, highpass and notch colours."],
 ["phase-distortion","phase-modulation","phasor","wavetable","modulation-matrix","modular","multimode-filter","web-audio","audioworklet"],
 note="Free-module walkthrough; download link mention dropped.")

mk("m6wfGX7rwiQ","Hybrid Orchestral Scoring: Synths + Strings + Percussion That Work Together",
 "hybrid-score-role-first-layering-synth-drone-string-swells-square-motor-dotted-pingpong-staggered-entry-reverse-render",
 "Role-first hybrid score layering: choose each part by its function (drone/pedal, rhythmic motor, lead, accompaniment, FX) and its frequency zone rather than by instrument family, then build a synth drone darkened via filter-envelope amount with mod-wheel string swells under it, a short square-wave motor with dotted-eighth ping-pong delay, staggered entries, render-to-audio and reversed hits with the transient trimmed",
 "Combine synths with strings and percussion so they support each other instead of piling up. First list the roles the cue needs (drone or pedal, rhythmic motor or ostinato, lead, harmonic accompaniment, effects) and pick, for each role, whichever source fits that function: string or synth, or both blended. Give each part its own frequency zone (bass, mid, top) and decide per pair whether to blend or separate. Concrete recipe: a big analog-style synth drone darkened by lowering the filter envelope amount first (then brightness) with more filter sustain, held for about 10 bars and rendered to audio to save CPU; tremolo string swells under it ridden on the mod wheel for movement; a motor from three detuned square oscillators (one up an octave) with no sustain and a short decay, into a tempo-synced ping-pong delay at a dotted eighth with more feedback, plus a little room reverb; bring ideas in one at a time; an arpeggiated version of the drone synth with short steps and envelope amount on a MIDI CC; and a rendered phrase reversed with its transient trimmed so it swells into the next entry. For generative systems this maps to role-tagged voices with frequency zones and entry order.",
 ["Any DAW or generative engine with a synth, a string (sample or synth string) layer, delay and reverb","Mod wheel or a controller fader (CC) for swells","Render/bounce-to-audio and reverse for clips"],
 [("roles","list","Functions the cue needs: drone/pedal, motor/ostinato, lead, accompaniment, FX",True),("tempo","number","Song tempo for synced delay",True),("zones","table","Frequency zone per role (low / mid / top)",True)],
 [("role_map","table","Role → chosen source → frequency zone → entry point"),("hybrid_cue","arrangement","Layered drone, motor, lead and reversed-swell cue")],
 ["Arrangement plus patch recipe. The video gives few numbers (about 10 bars, dotted eighth, CC21 as an example); set other amounts by ear.","Product, course and price talk dropped. Preset names not copied; 'effect-like huge reverb' is the portable alias for the specialty reverb used on the lead.","Do not bring all ideas in at once: the video calls that a classic mistake."],
 [("Write the role list first (drone/pedal, motor/ostinato, lead, accompaniment, FX). For each role choose the source by function: strings, synth, guitar, or a blend (for example a string ostinato doubled by a synth, or a drone that is synth plus strings). Assign each role a frequency zone so parts do not clash.","role map","A table of role → source → frequency zone"),
  ("Drone: load a big analog-style synth sound. To darken it, lower the filter envelope amount first (not just the cutoff), then reduce brightness, and raise the filter envelope sustain. Hold the chord for about 10 bars.","drone synth","A steady, darker drone that no longer brightens on each note"),
  ("Render the drone to audio and disable the synth track to save CPU, keeping it available to re-edit (for example to change key).","bounce","Same sound, lower CPU load"),
  ("Layer tremolo string swells under the drone, riding the mod wheel/controller fader so the strings come and go.","string swells","The drone gains movement as the strings swell in and out"),
  ("Motor: three oscillators (one an octave up), square wave, amp envelope with no sustain and a short decay, some detune. Add a tempo-synced ping-pong delay at a dotted eighth (a dotted quarter is the alternative), turn the delay feedback up, and add a little room reverb.","motor patch","A pulsing rhythmic motor whose echoes fill the gaps"),
  ("Stagger the entries: start with one or two ideas and bring the motor in later, instead of three ideas at once.","arrangement","Each new layer is noticed when it enters"),
  ("Lead: start from a sound close to what you want, check what the mod wheel does, and add a very large, effect-like reverb. Add a second motor by arpeggiating the drone synth with short steps and more envelope, map envelope amount to a MIDI CC (e.g. CC21) for live control, and keep its reverb moderate.","lead + arp","Lead sits in its own space; the arp brightness can be played live"),
  ("Line the bassline up with the drums (a cross-rhythm can work). Quantize loose parts to eighths, and keep the best of repeated takes.","rhythm","Bass and drums lock together"),
  ("Reverse swell: play a hit or phrase, render it to audio, reverse the clip, trim away the transient so only the 'shh' swell remains, and place it so it ends where the next drum element enters.","reverse render","A swell that leads into the next section without a click")],
 ["Every part in the role map has a role and a frequency zone, and no two loud parts share the same zone without a blend decision.","Lowering the filter envelope amount on the drone makes the note onsets less bright even with cutoff unchanged.","The motor's delayed repeats are on dotted eighths relative to the tempo.","The reversed clip has no audible click or transient at its end."],
 ["hybrid-score","arrangement","layering","drone","ostinato","ping-pong-delay","reverse-swell","frequency-zones","generative"],
 note="Long live-writing video; course promotion, product and price talk dropped.")

mk("7OatKTrtmJ8","How to Make a HUGE Reese Bass in Vital | Deep Dubstep & DnB Sound Design Tutorial",
 "reese-bass-three-saw-octave-down-mixed-unison-fine-detune-mono-legato-glide-softclip-parallel-comp-hpf-automation-mono-below-120",
 "Reese bass from three octave-down saws (outer two with 3-voice unison at about 10% detune and zero phase randomization, middle one single voice, outer pair fine-tuned slightly up and down), mono legato always-glide, soft-clip drive, half-mix compressor, resonant high-pass, low-cut reverb, soft-clip saturation, high roll-off, automated high-pass movement, mono below 120 Hz and kick/snare sidechain",
 "Build a wide, moving Reese bass for dark hybrid cues. Use three saw oscillators all one octave down: oscillators 1 and 3 with 3 unison voices, unison detune around 10% and phase randomization at 0; oscillator 2 a single voice. Fine-tune oscillator 1 slightly up and oscillator 3 slightly down for the slow beating. Set one voice (mono), glide about halfway, always-glide and legato on, and overlap MIDI notes to get slides. In the synth FX: soft-clip distortion at about 9 dB, then a compressor after it at about 50% mix (release ~36%, attack ~56% on its relative scale), an EQ with a slightly resonant high-pass, and a little short reverb with its low end removed. After the synth: soft-clip saturation (drive just over 3 dB, output −2 dB), an EQ rolling off the high end with a small mid dip, automated high-pass cutoff for movement, a slight pitch-wheel drift, mono below 120 Hz, and compressors sidechained to the kick and the snare.",
 ["Any synth with 3 oscillators, unison, mono/legato/glide and an FX rack (the video uses a free wavetable synth), or Web Audio: three detuned OscillatorNode groups → WaveShaper → DynamicsCompressor → BiquadFilter highpass with automated frequency (implementation note)","Post saturator and EQ with automation","Mono-below-frequency utility and sidechain compressors"],
 [("root_note","string","Bass note, e.g. F1 in the video",True),("unison_detune","number","About 10% on oscillators 1 and 3",True),("glide","number","About halfway, always-glide + legato",True),("mono_below_hz","number","120 Hz",False)],
 [("reese_patch","patch","Three-saw Reese with FX chain"),("automation","lanes","High-pass cutoff and pitch-wheel movement lanes")],
 ["Percent values are on the synth's own knob scale; treat them as starting points, not absolute times.","Keep everything below 120 Hz mono so the wide unison does not smear the low end.","Bass-music tutorial adapted for dark cinematic low end; subscribe talk dropped."],
 [("Oscillator 1: basic saw, −12 semitones, unison 3 voices, unison detune ~10%, phase randomization 0. Oscillator 2: saw, −12, one voice. Oscillator 3: same as oscillator 1.","3 saws","A thick, octave-down saw stack"),
  ("Fine-tune oscillator 1 slightly up and oscillator 3 slightly down.","fine detune","Slow beating/phasing movement typical of a Reese"),
  ("Set voices to 1 (mono), glide about halfway, enable always-glide and legato. Overlap the start of the next MIDI note with the end of the previous one to slide into it; separated notes do not bend.","mono glide","Smooth pitch slides between overlapping notes"),
  ("Synth FX: distortion in soft-clip mode at about 9 dB drive; compressor placed after the distortion with mix ~50%, release ~36%, attack ~56%.","drive + comp","Added grit with controlled, parallel-style dynamics"),
  ("Add an EQ with a high-pass filter, a touch of resonance, and the cutoff set into the low-mid. Add a small reverb with its low end removed and a shorter time.","eq + reverb","Cleaner low end and slight space without low mud"),
  ("After the synth: saturator in a soft-sine curve with soft clip on, drive just over 3 dB, output −2 dB. Then an EQ that rolls off the top end and makes a small mid dip.","post chain","Warmer, darker bass with headroom"),
  ("Automate the high-pass cutoff so it opens and closes over the phrase, and add a small pitch-wheel drift lane for extra movement. If it sounds muffled, ease the top roll-off back a little.","automation","The bass moves and breathes over time"),
  ("Make everything below 120 Hz mono and lower the gain slightly. Add two compressors sidechained to the kick and to the snare so the bass dips on each hit. Optionally resample and chop the result.","mono low + sidechain","Solid mono low end that makes room for drums")],
 ["Muting oscillators 1 and 3 leaves a thin single saw; un-muting brings back the wide beating.","Overlapping notes glide; non-overlapping notes start cleanly without a bend.","A correlation meter or mono check shows no low-end cancellation below 120 Hz.","The bass level dips audibly on kick and snare hits."],
 ["reese-bass","unison","detune","glide","soft-clip","sidechain","mono-low-end","dark-bass","web-audio"],
 note="Short tutorial; subscribe CTA dropped.")
